/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · Exodus chapters 11-15
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("exodus", [
{n:11, name:"The Final Warning", seal:{ref:"Exodus 11:7",verse:"But against any of the children of Israel shall not a dog move his tongue, against man or beast: that ye may know how that the LORD doth put a difference between the Egyptians and Israel."}, facts:["Did you know that Moses announced the final plague for about midnight, the stillest hour of the night, so that every Egyptian household would wake to the same cry at the same moment?","Did you know that when Moses said not even a dog would move its tongue against Israel, he meant the night would be so still for God's people that not even a bark would break it?","Did you know that the borrowing of silver and gold was really back wages? Israel had served Egypt for generations, and the LORD made the Egyptians pay them on the way out."], questions:[
{q:"How many more plagues did the LORD say He would bring upon Pharaoh and Egypt?", options:["One","Three","Seven","Twelve"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 11:1",verse:"And the LORD said unto Moses, Yet will I bring one plague more upon Pharaoh, and upon Egypt; afterwards he will let you go hence: when he shall let you go, he shall surely thrust you out hence altogether.", insight:"God's patience has a limit, but He announces the end before it comes. Even in judgment, He gives Pharaoh one last clear warning."},
{q:"What did the LORD say Pharaoh would do when he finally let the people go?", options:["He would surely thrust them out altogether","He would ask them to return within a month","He would send soldiers to guard them","He would hold back the women and children"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 11:1",verse:"And the LORD said unto Moses, Yet will I bring one plague more upon Pharaoh, and upon Egypt; afterwards he will let you go hence: when he shall let you go, he shall surely thrust you out hence altogether.", insight:"When God finally moves, the release is total. What Egypt clutched with a fist, it would fling away with an open hand."},
{q:"What was every man to borrow of his neighbour before leaving Egypt?", options:["Jewels of silver and jewels of gold","Weapons and shields","Oxen and wagons","Bread and water for the journey"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 11:2",verse:"Speak now in the ears of the people, and let every man borrow of his neighbour, and every woman of her neighbour, jewels of silver, and jewels of gold.", insight:"God provided for the journey before it began. Israel would not leave Egypt empty handed, because their Father had wages to pay them."},
{q:"In whose ears was Moses told to speak about borrowing the jewels?", options:["The ears of the people","The ears of Pharaoh's servants","The ears of the priests","The ears of the midwives"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 11:2",verse:"Speak now in the ears of the people, and let every man borrow of his neighbour, and every woman of her neighbour, jewels of silver, and jewels of gold.", insight:"God spoke His plan into the ears of ordinary people. The great deliverance was announced to the whole congregation, not just the leaders."},
{q:"Who gave the Israelites favour in the sight of the Egyptians?", options:["The LORD","Pharaoh himself","Joseph's household","The Hebrew midwives"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 11:3",verse:"And the LORD gave the people favour in the sight of the Egyptians. Moreover the man Moses was very great in the land of Egypt, in the sight of Pharaoh’s servants, and in the sight of the people.", insight:"Favour is God's doing, not man's earning. He turned the hearts of Egypt's people toward Israel at exactly the right hour."},
{q:"How was Moses described in the land of Egypt at this time?", options:["He was very great, in the sight of Pharaoh's servants and the people","He was hidden among the Hebrews","He was imprisoned in Pharaoh's palace","He was forgotten by the Egyptians"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 11:3",verse:"And the LORD gave the people favour in the sight of the Egyptians. Moreover the man Moses was very great in the land of Egypt, in the sight of Pharaoh’s servants, and in the sight of the people.", insight:"Faithfulness made Moses great in a foreign land. A shepherd with a staff outshone a king with an army, because God was with him."},
{q:"At what time did Moses say the LORD would go out into the midst of Egypt?", options:["About midnight","At sunrise","At noon","At the evening sacrifice"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 11:4",verse:"And Moses said, Thus saith the LORD, About midnight will I go out into the midst of Egypt:", insight:"God keeps His appointments to the hour. Midnight, the darkest watch, became the moment of His greatest deliverance."},
{q:"Whose words was Moses speaking when he announced the plague to Pharaoh?", options:["Thus saith the LORD","Thus saith Moses","Thus saith Aaron","Thus saith the elders"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 11:4",verse:"And Moses said, Thus saith the LORD, About midnight will I go out into the midst of Egypt:", insight:"Moses never spoke on his own authority. Thus saith the LORD is the only ground firm enough to stand on when announcing hard truth."},
{q:"Whose firstborn would die in the final plague?", options:["From Pharaoh's firstborn on his throne to the maidservant's behind the mill, and the firstborn of beasts","Only Pharaoh's firstborn son","Only the priests of Egypt","Only the firstborn of the cattle"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 11:5",verse:"And all the firstborn in the land of Egypt shall die, from the firstborn of Pharaoh that sitteth upon his throne, even unto the firstborn of the maidservant that is behind the mill; and all the firstborn of beasts.", insight:"No rank escapes God's judgment, from throne to millstone. Yet His justice and His mercy both reach everywhere."},
{q:"Behind what did the maidservant sit, according to the warning of the final plague?", options:["The mill","The oven","The loom","The throne"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 11:5",verse:"And all the firstborn in the land of Egypt shall die, from the firstborn of Pharaoh that sitteth upon his throne, even unto the firstborn of the maidservant that is behind the mill; and all the firstborn of beasts.", insight:"God's warning names the lowliest worker by name, as it were. His judgment notices the servant girl behind the mill as surely as the king on the throne."},
{q:"What would be heard throughout all the land of Egypt after the plague?", options:["A great cry, such as there was none like it","Songs of celebration","The sound of chariots","Silence like a desert"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 11:6",verse:"And there shall be a great cry throughout all the land of Egypt, such as there was none like it, nor shall be like it any more.", insight:"Sin's harvest is always louder than its sowing. Egypt had filled Goshen with cries of bondage; now the cry came home."},
{q:"How did Moses describe the coming cry in Egypt?", options:["Such as there was none like it, nor shall be like it any more","Like the cry at the Red Sea","Like the wailing of the Nile","Like thunder on Sinai"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 11:6",verse:"And there shall be a great cry throughout all the land of Egypt, such as there was none like it, nor shall be like it any more.", insight:"Some judgments are without precedent and without repeat. God wants this night remembered as utterly unique in history."},
{q:"What would not even a dog do against any of the children of Israel?", options:["Move his tongue","Bark at the door","Leave his master's house","Touch their flocks"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 11:7",verse:"But against any of the children of Israel shall not a dog move his tongue, against man or beast: that ye may know how that the LORD doth put a difference between the Egyptians and Israel.", insight:"God's protection is so complete that not even a dog's bark would disturb His people that night. When He guards, the night itself keeps silence."},
{q:"Why would the LORD put a difference between the Egyptians and Israel?", options:["That they might know the LORD makes a distinction between them","That Egypt might envy Israel","That Israel might fear the night","That Pharaoh might be entertained"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 11:7",verse:"But against any of the children of Israel shall not a dog move his tongue, against man or beast: that ye may know how that the LORD doth put a difference between the Egyptians and Israel.", insight:"God marks the difference between His own and the world. The distinction is His work, not our worthiness, and it stands in the darkest night."},
{q:"What would Pharaoh's servants do after the plague, according to Moses?", options:["Come down, bow down, and say, Get thee out","Build a wall around Goshen","Seize the Israelites' gold","Flee to the Red Sea"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 11:8",verse:"And all these thy servants shall come down unto me, and bow down themselves unto me, saying, Get thee out, and all the people that follow thee: and after that I will go out. And he went out from Pharaoh in a great anger.", insight:"Pride bows eventually. The servants who had mocked Moses would beg him to leave, because God's hand leaves no room for argument."},
{q:"In what manner did Moses go out from Pharaoh?", options:["In a great anger","Weeping","Fearful and trembling","Singing a psalm"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 11:8",verse:"And all these thy servants shall come down unto me, and bow down themselves unto me, saying, Get thee out, and all the people that follow thee: and after that I will go out. And he went out from Pharaoh in a great anger.", insight:"There is a holy anger that burns against stubborn evil. Moses left Pharaoh's court carrying the weight of a judgment he had begged the king to avoid."},
{q:"Why would Pharaoh not hearken unto Moses and Aaron?", options:["That the LORD's wonders might be multiplied in Egypt","Because the servants persuaded him","Because the frogs returned","Because Moses spoke too softly"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 11:9",verse:"And the LORD said unto Moses, Pharaoh shall not hearken unto you; that my wonders may be multiplied in the land of Egypt.", insight:"God used Pharaoh's stubbornness to multiply His wonders. Even a hard heart becomes a canvas for His glory."},
{q:"What did the LORD say Pharaoh would do in response to Moses' words?", options:["He shall not hearken unto you","He shall repent at once","He shall free the slaves","He shall send gifts to Israel"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 11:9",verse:"And the LORD said unto Moses, Pharaoh shall not hearken unto you; that my wonders may be multiplied in the land of Egypt.", insight:"God told Moses the outcome in advance so he would not despair at Pharaoh's refusal. Knowing God's plan steadies us when people will not listen."},
{q:"Who did all these wonders before Pharaoh?", options:["Moses and Aaron","Moses alone","Aaron alone","The elders of Israel"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 11:10",verse:"And Moses and Aaron did all these wonders before Pharaoh: and the LORD hardened Pharaoh’s heart, so that he would not let the children of Israel go out of his land.", insight:"Obedience is the channel of wonders. Moses and Aaron simply did what God said, and heaven did the rest."},
{q:"Why did Pharaoh not let the children of Israel go out of his land?", options:["The LORD hardened Pharaoh's heart","The Egyptians hid the roads","The Israelites refused to leave","The cattle were not ready"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 11:10",verse:"And Moses and Aaron did all these wonders before Pharaoh: and the LORD hardened Pharaoh’s heart, so that he would not let the children of Israel go out of his land.", insight:"A heart can harden past the point of return. Pharaoh's story warns us to answer God while His voice still stirs us."}
]},
{n:12, name:"The Passover", seal:{ref:"Exodus 12:13",verse:"And the blood shall be to you for a token upon the houses where ye are: and when I see the blood, I will pass over you, and the plague shall not be upon you to destroy you, when I smite the land of Egypt."}, facts:["Did you know the word Passover comes from the Hebrew pesach, which means to pass over, or to hover protectively like a bird guarding its nest?","Did you know Israel ate the Passover with their loins girded, shoes on, and staff in hand, the posture of travelers ready to march at a moment's notice?","Did you know the sojourning of Israel in Egypt lasted 430 years, and they went out on the very selfsame day the years were fulfilled?"], questions:[
{q:"What would this month be unto Israel?", options:["The beginning of months","The month of harvest","The last month of the year","The month of trumpets"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 12:2",verse:"This month shall be unto you the beginning of months: it shall be the first month of the year to you.", insight:"Redemption resets the calendar. For Israel, time itself began again the month God brought them out."},
{q:"On which day of the month was each household to take a lamb?", options:["The tenth day","The first day","The seventh day","The fourteenth day"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 12:3",verse:"Speak ye unto all the congregation of Israel, saying, In the tenth day of this month they shall take to them every man a lamb, according to the house of their fathers, a lamb for an house:", insight:"God gave four days to look the lamb over. The sacrifice had to be examined and found flawless, because only perfection could picture Christ."},
{q:"What kind of lamb was required for the Passover?", options:["Without blemish, a male of the first year","Any lamb taken from the flock","A lamb of the second year","A spotted or speckled lamb"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 12:5",verse:"Your lamb shall be without blemish, a male of the first year: ye shall take it out from the sheep, or from the goats:", insight:"God accepts no blemished offering. The spotless lamb pointed to a spotless Saviour, examined and found without fault."},
{q:"When was the lamb to be killed?", options:["In the evening of the fourteenth day","At noon on the tenth day","At sunrise on the first day","At midnight on the seventh day"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 12:6",verse:"And ye shall keep it up until the fourteenth day of the same month: and the whole assembly of the congregation of Israel shall kill it in the evening.", insight:"God set the very hour of the sacrifice. The lamb died at evening, in the quiet close of day, when every household was gathered."},
{q:"Where was the blood of the lamb to be put?", options:["On the lintel and the two side posts","On the threshold only","On the roof of the house","On the door of the tent"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 12:7",verse:"And they shall take of the blood, and strike it on the two side posts and on the upper door post of the houses, wherein they shall eat it.", insight:"The blood had to be applied, not just shed. Salvation is personal: the lamb died for the household, and the blood marked the door."},
{q:"What was to be eaten with the lamb that night?", options:["Unleavened bread and bitter herbs","Leavened bread and honey","Roasted grain and figs","Boiled meat and wine"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 12:8",verse:"And they shall eat the flesh in that night, roast with fire, and unleavened bread; and with bitter herbs they shall eat it.", insight:"Bitter herbs kept the memory of bondage sharp, and unleavened bread spoke of haste and purity. God seasons freedom with remembrance."},
{q:"How were they to eat it, with what on their feet?", options:["With their shoes on their feet","With bare feet","With sandals in hand","With staffs on the ground"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 12:11",verse:"And thus shall ye eat it; with your loins girded, your shoes on your feet, and your staff in your hand; and ye shall eat it in haste: it is the LORD’S passover.", insight:"They ate dressed for the road. Faith eats its meals ready to move the moment God says go."},
{q:"What was to be in their hand as they ate in haste?", options:["A staff","A sword","A torch","A harp"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 12:11",verse:"And thus shall ye eat it; with your loins girded, your shoes on your feet, and your staff in your hand; and ye shall eat it in haste: it is the LORD’S passover.", insight:"The staff was a traveler's tool, not a warrior's sword. Israel's deliverance would be God's work; their part was to be ready."},
{q:"What would the LORD do to the gods of Egypt that night?", options:["Execute judgment against them","Carry them into Goshen","Set them upon the altar","Spare them alone of all"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 12:12",verse:"For I will pass through the land of Egypt this night, and will smite all the firstborn in the land of Egypt, both man and beast; and against all the gods of Egypt I will execute judgment: I am the LORD.", insight:"The plagues were never only about Pharaoh. That night God judged Egypt's gods too, showing every idol powerless before Him."},
{q:"What did the LORD say He would do when He saw the blood?", options:["Pass over them","Enter the house","Strike the lintel","Call the destroyer back"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 12:13",verse:"And the blood shall be to you for a token upon the houses where ye are: and when I see the blood, I will pass over you, and the plague shall not be upon you to destroy you, when I smite the land of Egypt.", insight:"God's promise hangs on two words: I will. When He sees the blood, He passes over. The ground of safety is His word, not our worth."},
{q:"How long was the Passover to be kept as a memorial?", options:["Forever, by an ordinance","For seven generations","Until the wilderness journey","For one hundred years"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 12:14",verse:"And this day shall be unto you for a memorial; and ye shall keep it a feast to the LORD throughout your generations; ye shall keep it a feast by an ordinance for ever.", insight:"God wants His mercies remembered, not just received. The memorial feast turned one night's rescue into generations of gratitude."},
{q:"How many days were they to eat unleavened bread?", options:["Seven","Three","Ten","Forty"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 12:15",verse:"Seven days shall ye eat unleavened bread; even the first day ye shall put away leaven out of your houses: for whosoever eateth leavened bread from the first day until the seventh day, that soul shall be cut off from Israel.", insight:"Seven days of unleavened bread taught a week-long lesson: a redeemed life leaves the old leaven behind, completely."},
{q:"What was to happen on the first day of the feast?", options:["Leaven was to be put away out of their houses","The lamb was to be chosen","The journey was to begin","The trumpets were to sound"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 12:15",verse:"Seven days shall ye eat unleavened bread; even the first day ye shall put away leaven out of your houses: for whosoever eateth leavened bread from the first day until the seventh day, that soul shall be cut off from Israel.", insight:"Leaven had to go on day one. God does not ask us to nibble at holiness gradually; He asks for a clean house from the start."},
{q:"With what were they to strike the lintel and the side posts?", options:["A bunch of hyssop dipped in the blood","A palm branch","Their bare hands","A linen cloth"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 12:22",verse:"And ye shall take a bunch of hyssop, and dip it in the blood that is in the bason, and strike the lintel and the two side posts with the blood that is in the bason; and none of you shall go out at the door of his house until the morning.", insight:"A humble bunch of hyssop carried the blood to the door. God often uses the lowliest tools to apply His greatest mercies."},
{q:"Whom would the LORD not allow to enter the blood-marked house to smite them?", options:["The destroyer","The captain of Egypt","The spirit of the night","The angel of the sea"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 12:23",verse:"For the LORD will pass through to smite the Egyptians; and when he seeth the blood upon the lintel, and on the two side posts, the LORD will pass over the door, and will not suffer the destroyer to come in unto your houses to smite you.", insight:"The destroyer stopped at the blood-marked door. Obedience to God's appointed way is the only shelter that holds."},
{q:"What answer were the fathers to give when their children asked, What mean ye by this service?", options:["It is the sacrifice of the LORD's passover","It is the feast of harvest","It is the offering of firstfruits","It is the memorial of manna"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 12:27",verse:"That ye shall say, It is the sacrifice of the LORD’S passover, who passed over the houses of the children of Israel in Egypt, when he smote the Egyptians, and delivered our houses. And the people bowed the head and worshipped.", insight:"God built questions into the feast. When children ask what mean ye, faith gets passed to the next generation at the table."},
{q:"At what time did the LORD smite the firstborn of Egypt?", options:["At midnight","At evening","At dawn","At noon"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 12:29",verse:"And it came to pass, that at midnight the LORD smote all the firstborn in the land of Egypt, from the firstborn of Pharaoh that sat on his throne unto the firstborn of the captive that was in the dungeon; and all the firstborn of cattle.", insight:"At midnight the word became deed. God's promises are not poetry; they arrive on schedule, in real time, in real houses."},
{q:"What was true of every Egyptian house that night?", options:["There was not a house where there was not one dead","Every house was burned","Every door was broken","Every lamp was lit"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 12:30",verse:"And Pharaoh rose up in the night, he, and all his servants, and all the Egyptians; and there was a great cry in Egypt; for there was not a house where there was not one dead.", insight:"Judgment fell house by house, with no exceptions for rank. The night proved that no one is beyond God's reach, for mercy or for justice."},
{q:"What did Pharaoh say to Moses and Aaron in the night?", options:["Rise up, and get you forth from among my people","Come and serve in my palace","Bring me your gold","Return to your work"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 12:31",verse:"And he called for Moses and Aaron by night, and said, Rise up, and get you forth from among my people, both ye and the children of Israel; and go, serve the LORD, as ye have said.", insight:"The king who said who is the LORD now begged Israel to go. God's power turns defiance into surrender, sooner or later."},
{q:"How many men journeyed from Rameses to Succoth, besides children?", options:["About six hundred thousand on foot","About sixty thousand","About six thousand","About six million"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 12:37",verse:"And the children of Israel journeyed from Rameses to Succoth, about six hundred thousand on foot that were men, beside children.", insight:"About six hundred thousand men marched out, besides women and children. What left Egypt as a crowd of slaves arrived at Sinai as a nation."}
]},
{n:13, name:"Pillar of Cloud and Fire", seal:{ref:"Exodus 13:21",verse:"And the LORD went before them by day in a pillar of a cloud, to lead them the way; and by night in a pillar of fire, to give them light; to go by day and night:"}, facts:["Did you know the month Abib, when Israel left Egypt, means the month of green ears, named for the young barley ripening in the fields?","Did you know Joseph's bones traveled with Israel all the way through the wilderness? He had made them swear it, trusting that God would surely visit them.","Did you know God chose the longer desert road on purpose? He knew the sight of war on the near Philistine road would send a newborn nation running back to Egypt."], questions:[
{q:"Whom was Moses to sanctify unto the LORD?", options:["All the firstborn, both of man and of beast","All the elders of Israel","All the firstborn of Pharaoh","All the priests of Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 13:2",verse:"Sanctify unto me all the firstborn, whatsoever openeth the womb among the children of Israel, both of man and of beast: it is mine.", insight:"The firstborn belonged to God because He had spared them. What He saves, He claims; redemption always ends in consecration."},
{q:"What day were the people commanded to remember?", options:["The day they came out from Egypt, out of the house of bondage","The day of the first rain","The day Joseph died","The day of the new moon"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 13:3",verse:"And Moses said unto the people, Remember this day, in which ye came out from Egypt, out of the house of bondage; for by strength of hand the LORD brought you out from this place: there shall no leavened bread be eaten.", insight:"Memory is a duty, not a feeling. God commanded Israel to remember, because forgetfulness is the first step back to bondage."},
{q:"In what month did they go out of Egypt?", options:["Abib","Ethanim","Bul","Adar"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 13:4",verse:"This day came ye out in the month Abib.", insight:"God anchored the exodus to a real month in a real year. Our faith rests on history, not on myth."},
{q:"When the LORD brought them into the land of the Canaanites, what were they to keep?", options:["This service in this month","The service of the tabernacle","The service of the priests","The service of the Levites"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 13:5",verse:"And it shall be when the LORD shall bring thee into the land of the Canaanites, and the Hittites, and the Amorites, and the Hivites, and the Jebusites, which he sware unto thy fathers to give thee, a land flowing with milk and honey, that thou shalt keep this service in this month.", insight:"The feast was for the future, not just the past. God wanted the story told in Canaan, where the promise would be fulfilled."},
{q:"What was to be seen with thee during the seven days of the feast?", options:["No leavened bread, neither leaven seen in all thy quarters","No meat of any kind","No water in the vessels","No fire upon the altar"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 13:7",verse:"Unleavened bread shall be eaten seven days; and there shall no leavened bread be seen with thee, neither shall there be leaven seen with thee in all thy quarters.", insight:"Not even a trace of leaven could remain in sight. God wants sin not just reduced, but removed from view."},
{q:"What were fathers to tell their sons about the feast?", options:["It is because of that which the LORD did for me when I came forth out of Egypt","It is because of Joseph's bones","It is because of the pillar of fire","It is because of the manna"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 13:8",verse:"And thou shalt shew thy son in that day, saying, This is done because of that which the LORD did unto me when I came forth out of Egypt.", insight:"Every father was a preacher at that table. The story of redemption was meant to be told personally: the LORD did this for me."},
{q:"Where was the feast to be for a sign?", options:["Upon thine hand","Upon the doorpost","Upon the altar","Upon the priest's forehead"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 13:9",verse:"And it shall be for a sign unto thee upon thine hand, and for a memorial between thine eyes, that the LORD’S law may be in thy mouth: for with a strong hand hath the LORD brought thee out of Egypt.", insight:"What we do with our hands should show what God has done for us. The feast was to mark their actions, not just their memory."},
{q:"Where was it to be for a memorial?", options:["Between thine eyes","Behind the door","Under the pillow","Upon the city gate"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 13:9",verse:"And it shall be for a sign unto thee upon thine hand, and for a memorial between thine eyes, that the LORD’S law may be in thy mouth: for with a strong hand hath the LORD brought thee out of Egypt.", insight:"Between the eyes means always in view. God's mercies should shape how we see everything."},
{q:"When were they to keep this ordinance?", options:["In his season from year to year","At every new moon","On every sabbath","Once in a lifetime"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 13:10",verse:"Thou shalt therefore keep this ordinance in his season from year to year.", insight:"Year by year, in its season. Faithfulness is rhythmic, not random; God builds His people through repeated remembrance."},
{q:"What was to be set apart unto the LORD?", options:["Every firstborn male, both of man and of cattle","Every seventh lamb","Every first sheaf of grain","Every new garment"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 13:12",verse:"That thou shalt set apart unto the LORD all that openeth the matrix, and every firstling that cometh of a beast which thou hast; the males shall be the LORD’S.", insight:"The first and best belong to God. Setting apart the firstborn trained Israel to give God the first of everything."},
{q:"With what was the firstling of an ass to be redeemed?", options:["With a lamb","With silver","With a dove","With fine flour"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 13:13",verse:"And every firstling of an ass thou shalt redeem with a lamb; and if thou wilt not redeem it, then thou shalt break his neck: and all the firstborn of man among thy children shalt thou redeem.", insight:"Even a donkey's firstborn had to be redeemed with a lamb. God left nothing outside the reach of redemption's price."},
{q:"What was to be done to the firstling of an ass if it was not redeemed?", options:["Its neck was to be broken","It was to be set free","It was to be sold","It was to be burned"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 13:13",verse:"And every firstling of an ass thou shalt redeem with a lamb; and if thou wilt not redeem it, then thou shalt break his neck: and all the firstborn of man among thy children shalt thou redeem.", insight:"Refusing redemption had a cost. The unredeemed firstling died, a sober picture of life without the Lamb."},
{q:"When a son asked, What is this, how was the answer to begin?", options:["By strength of hand the LORD brought us out from Egypt","By the wisdom of Moses","By the riches of Goshen","By the chariots of Israel"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 13:14",verse:"And it shall be when thy son asketh thee in time to come, saying, What is this? that thou shalt say unto him, By strength of hand the LORD brought us out from Egypt, from the house of bondage:", insight:"The answer to the child's question was God's strength, not Israel's. Every generation must learn that salvation is His work."},
{q:"Why did God not lead them through the way of the land of the Philistines?", options:["Lest the people repent when they see war, and return to Egypt","Because the Philistines were too strong","Because the road was flooded","Because Pharaoh blocked it"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 13:17",verse:"And it came to pass, when Pharaoh had let the people go, that God led them not through the way of the land of the Philistines, although that was near; for God said, Lest peradventure the people repent when they see war, and they return to Egypt:", insight:"God knows our frame. He led young Israel around the battle, because He measures the road to the traveler's strength."},
{q:"How was the way of the Philistines described?", options:["Near","Far","Impassable","Forgotten"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 13:17",verse:"And it came to pass, when Pharaoh had let the people go, that God led them not through the way of the land of the Philistines, although that was near; for God said, Lest peradventure the people repent when they see war, and they return to Egypt:", insight:"The shortest road is not always God's road. Near looked efficient, but God saw the war it would bring."},
{q:"Which way did God lead them instead?", options:["About, through the way of the wilderness of the Red sea","Straight through Canaan","Back toward Egypt","Around Mount Sinai"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 13:18",verse:"But God led the people about, through the way of the wilderness of the Red sea: and the children of Israel went up harnessed out of the land of Egypt.", insight:"God chose the longer way on purpose. The detour was not delay; it was mercy with a map."},
{q:"Whose bones did Moses take with him out of Egypt?", options:["Joseph's","Jacob's","Abraham's","Aaron's"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 13:19",verse:"And Moses took the bones of Joseph with him: for he had straitly sworn the children of Israel, saying, God will surely visit you; and ye shall carry up my bones away hence with you.", insight:"Joseph believed God's promise from inside a coffin in Egypt. Centuries later, his bones rode out with the exodus he had foreseen."},
{q:"What oath had Joseph made the children of Israel swear?", options:["God will surely visit you, and ye shall carry up my bones away hence with you","They would never leave Egypt","They would bury him in Canaan alone","They would remember his dreams"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 13:19",verse:"And Moses took the bones of Joseph with him: for he had straitly sworn the children of Israel, saying, God will surely visit you; and ye shall carry up my bones away hence with you.", insight:"Joseph made them swear, and they kept the oath. Promises outlive the people who make them when God is the witness."},
{q:"What went before the people by day?", options:["A pillar of a cloud","A pillar of smoke","A flock of doves","A burning bush"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 13:21",verse:"And the LORD went before them by day in a pillar of a cloud, to lead them the way; and by night in a pillar of fire, to give them light; to go by day and night:", insight:"God Himself went before them, visible by day. His people never marched alone; the Guide walked at the head of the column."},
{q:"What did the LORD never take away from before the people?", options:["The pillar of cloud by day and of fire by night","The manna that fell from heaven","The bones of Joseph","The ark of the covenant"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 13:22",verse:"He took not away the pillar of the cloud by day, nor the pillar of fire by night, from before the people.", insight:"The pillar never left its post. God's guidance is not a passing visit; it stays with His people through every mile."}
]},
{n:14, name:"The Red Sea", seal:{ref:"Exodus 14:14",verse:"The LORD shall fight for you, and ye shall hold your peace."}, facts:["Did you know the crossing came by a strong east wind blowing all night? The LORD used the ordinary forces of His creation to open an extraordinary road.","Did you know Pharaoh's six hundred chosen chariots were the pride of Egypt's army, and the sea closed over every one of them?","Did you know that the LORD shall fight for you, and ye shall hold your peace, is one of the Bible's great battle cries? Israel's only weapon that day was trust."], questions:[
{q:"Where did the LORD tell Israel to encamp?", options:["Before Pi-hahiroth, between Migdol and the sea, over against Baal-zephon","At the foot of Sinai","Beside the Jordan","In the plains of Moab"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 14:2",verse:"Speak unto the children of Israel, that they turn and encamp before Pi-hahiroth, between Migdol and the sea, over against Baal-zephon: before it shall ye encamp by the sea.", insight:"God placed Israel exactly where He wanted them: hemmed in, with the sea ahead and Pharaoh behind. The trap was His design, not their mistake."},
{q:"What would Pharaoh say about Israel?", options:["They are entangled in the land, the wilderness hath shut them in","They are mighty warriors","They have vanished like smoke","They march in perfect order"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 14:3",verse:"For Pharaoh will say of the children of Israel, They are entangled in the land, the wilderness hath shut them in.", insight:"Pharaoh read the map and smiled. He mistook God's strategy for Israel's blunder, and that mistake cost him his army."},
{q:"How did Pharaoh's heart change after Israel left?", options:["It was turned, and he asked, Why have we let Israel go from serving us","It softened toward Moses","It broke in repentance","It was filled with joy"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 14:5",verse:"And it was told the king of Egypt that the people fled: and the heart of Pharaoh and of his servants was turned against the people, and they said, Why have we done this, that we have let Israel go from serving us?", insight:"Freedom granted in fear is regretted in pride. Pharaoh's heart turned the moment the plagues stopped hurting."},
{q:"What did Pharaoh make ready for the pursuit?", options:["His chariot","His crown","His fleet of ships","His royal tent"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 14:6",verse:"And he made ready his chariot, and took his people with him:", insight:"Pharaoh harnessed his own chariot. When pride takes the reins personally, the fall is never far behind."},
{q:"How many chosen chariots did Pharaoh take?", options:["Six hundred","Sixty","Six thousand","Six"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 14:7",verse:"And he took six hundred chosen chariots, and all the chariots of Egypt, and captains over every one of them.", insight:"Six hundred chosen chariots was Egypt's finest hour, and its last. Humanity's best weapons are no match for God's purposes."},
{q:"Who rode with the chariots?", options:["Captains over every one of them","Pharaoh riding alone","The magicians","Foot soldiers only"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 14:7",verse:"And he took six hundred chosen chariots, and all the chariots of Egypt, and captains over every one of them.", insight:"Every chariot had its captain, and every captain had his orders. Rank and organization mean nothing when God commands the sea."},
{q:"What did Israel do when they saw the Egyptians marching after them?", options:["They were sore afraid, and cried out unto the LORD","They picked up stones to fight","They ran into the sea","They surrendered at once"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 14:10",verse:"And when Pharaoh drew nigh, the children of Israel lifted up their eyes, and, behold, the Egyptians marched after them; and they were sore afraid: and the children of Israel cried out unto the LORD.", insight:"Fear drove them to the right place at last: they cried out to the LORD. Panic becomes prayer when it turns Godward."},
{q:"What bitter question did the people ask Moses?", options:["Because there were no graves in Egypt, hast thou taken us away to die in the wilderness?","Where is the promised land?","Why did you bring no swords?","When will the manna come?"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 14:11",verse:"And they said unto Moses, Because there were no graves in Egypt, hast thou taken us away to die in the wilderness? wherefore hast thou dealt thus with us, to carry us forth out of Egypt?", insight:"One glimpse of trouble, and they rewrote the whole story. Fear makes us forget ten plagues of proof in a single afternoon."},
{q:"What had the people said back in Egypt?", options:["Let us alone, that we may serve the Egyptians","Send us to Canaan at once","Give us chariots too","We will fight Pharaoh ourselves"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 14:12",verse:"Is not this the word that we did tell thee in Egypt, saying, Let us alone, that we may serve the Egyptians? For it had been better for us to serve the Egyptians, than that we should die in the wilderness.", insight:"They preferred the chains they knew to the freedom they feared. The slavery of the familiar can feel safer than the liberty of the unknown."},
{q:"What three commands did Moses give the people?", options:["Fear ye not, stand still, and see the salvation of the LORD","Run, hide, and be silent","Fight, pray, and flee","March, sing, and shout"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 14:13",verse:"And Moses said unto the people, Fear ye not, stand still, and see the salvation of the LORD, which he will shew to you to day: for the Egyptians whom ye have seen to day, ye shall see them again no more for ever.", insight:"God's strangest battle plan: do nothing but watch. Sometimes the most spiritual thing you can do is stand still and look."},
{q:"Who would fight for Israel?", options:["The LORD shall fight for you, and ye shall hold your peace","Moses with his rod","Aaron with his censer","The captains of Israel"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 14:14",verse:"The LORD shall fight for you, and ye shall hold your peace.", insight:"Israel's assignment was silence; God's was war. We hold our peace so that He can hold the field."},
{q:"What did the LORD tell Moses to command the people to do?", options:["Go forward","Turn back","Build an altar","Dig trenches"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 14:15",verse:"And the LORD said unto Moses, Wherefore criest thou unto me? speak unto the children of Israel, that they go forward:", insight:"Prayer has its place, and then marching has its. God told Moses to stop crying and start walking, straight toward the sea."},
{q:"What was Moses to stretch his hand over?", options:["The sea, to divide it","The sky, to darken it","The camp, to bless it","The chariots, to stop them"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 14:16",verse:"But lift thou up thy rod, and stretch out thine hand over the sea, and divide it: and the children of Israel shall go on dry ground through the midst of the sea.", insight:"The rod did not part the sea; obedience did. Moses stretched out his hand, and God stretched out His power."},
{q:"What moved from before the camp of Israel to behind it?", options:["The angel of God, and the pillar of the cloud","The ark of the covenant","The twelve tribes","The flocks and herds"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 14:19",verse:"And the angel of God, which went before the camp of Israel, removed and went behind them; and the pillar of the cloud went from before their face, and stood behind them:", insight:"God moved from leading to guarding, placing Himself between His people and their enemy. He covers both the march and the rear."},
{q:"What was the cloud to the Egyptians, and to Israel?", options:["Darkness to the Egyptians, but light by night to Israel","Fire to both camps","Rain upon both","Invisible to both"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 14:20",verse:"And it came between the camp of the Egyptians and the camp of Israel; and it was a cloud and darkness to them, but it gave light by night to these: so that the one came not near the other all the night.", insight:"The same cloud was darkness to Egypt and light to Israel. God's presence is comfort to His own and confusion to His foes."},
{q:"How did the LORD drive the sea back all that night?", options:["With a strong east wind","With a mighty earthquake","With a wall of fire","With a sudden frost"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 14:21",verse:"And Moses stretched out his hand over the sea; and the LORD caused the sea to go back by a strong east wind all that night, and made the sea dry land, and the waters were divided.", insight:"All night the wind blew, unseen hands doing visible work. God often works the night shift, preparing morning's miracle."},
{q:"What were the waters to Israel as they crossed?", options:["A wall unto them on their right hand and on their left","A bridge of stone","A shallow stream","A mist that hid them"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 14:22",verse:"And the children of Israel went into the midst of the sea upon the dry ground: and the waters were a wall unto them on their right hand, and on their left.", insight:"Water stood like walls on both sides. God's deliverance can look terrifyingly close, yet never touch His people."},
{q:"What did the Egyptians cry when their chariot wheels came off?", options:["Let us flee from the face of Israel, for the LORD fighteth for them","Let us charge the camp","Let us mend the wheels","Let us call Pharaoh"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 14:25",verse:"And took off their chariot wheels, that they drave them heavily: so that the Egyptians said, Let us flee from the face of Israel; for the LORD fighteth for them against the Egyptians.", insight:"Egypt's soldiers finally preached the sermon: the LORD fighteth for them. The enemy's confession came too late to save them."},
{q:"What happened to Pharaoh's host that followed into the sea?", options:["The waters covered them, and there remained not so much as one of them","They turned back safely","They crossed to the other side","They were taken prisoner"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 14:28",verse:"And the waters returned, and covered the chariots, and the horsemen, and all the host of Pharaoh that came into the sea after them; there remained not so much as one of them.", insight:"Not one of Pharaoh's host remained. God's victories are complete; He does not do half deliverances."},
{q:"What did Israel do when they saw the great work at the sea?", options:["They feared the LORD, and believed the LORD and his servant Moses","They crowned Moses king","They returned to Egypt","They built a golden calf"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 14:31",verse:"And Israel saw that great work which the LORD did upon the Egyptians: and the people feared the LORD, and believed the LORD, and his servant Moses.", insight:"Seeing led to fearing, and fearing led to believing. The sea that drowned Egypt baptized Israel into trust."}
]},
{n:15, name:"The Song of Moses", seal:{ref:"Exodus 15:2",verse:"The LORD is my strength and song, and he is become my salvation: he is my God, and I will prepare him an habitation; my father’s God, and I will exalt him."}, facts:["Did you know the Song of Moses is the first song in the Bible sung by a whole redeemed nation, with men and women answering one another across the shore?","Did you know Marah means bitter? The tree God showed Moses turned the bitter pool sweet, a picture of how His healing reaches the bitter places in us.","Did you know Elim held twelve wells of water and seventy palm trees, a green resting place with room for every tribe after three thirsty days?"], questions:[
{q:"How did the song of Moses begin?", options:["I will sing unto the LORD, for he hath triumphed gloriously","I will weep before the LORD","I will build an altar to the LORD","I will march behind the LORD"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 15:1",verse:"Then sang Moses and the children of Israel this song unto the LORD, and spake, saying, I will sing unto the LORD, for he hath triumphed gloriously: the horse and his rider hath he thrown into the sea.", insight:"The first song of the redeemed is pure praise. Before Israel built anything, they sang; gratitude is the foundation of worship."},
{q:"What had the LORD thrown into the sea?", options:["The horse and his rider","The chariots of iron","The crown of Pharaoh","The gods of Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 15:1",verse:"Then sang Moses and the children of Israel this song unto the LORD, and spake, saying, I will sing unto the LORD, for he hath triumphed gloriously: the horse and his rider hath he thrown into the sea.", insight:"The song names the victory plainly: horse and rider thrown into the sea. True praise is specific about what God has done."},
{q:"What did Moses call the LORD in the song?", options:["My strength and song, and my salvation","My shield and fortress","My light in darkness","My shepherd and guide"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 15:2",verse:"The LORD is my strength and song, and he is become my salvation: he is my God, and I will prepare him an habitation; my father’s God, and I will exalt him.", insight:"Moses calls the LORD his strength and his song. God is both the power that saves and the music that celebrates it."},
{q:"What is the LORD called in verse 3?", options:["A man of war","A prince of peace","A father of mercy","A king of kings"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 15:3",verse:"The LORD is a man of war: the LORD is his name.", insight:"The LORD is a man of war: not cruel, but courageous for His people. He fights the battles His children cannot."},
{q:"Where were Pharaoh's chosen captains drowned?", options:["In the Red sea","In the river Nile","In the wilderness","In the Jordan"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 15:4",verse:"Pharaoh’s chariots and his host hath he cast into the sea: his chosen captains also are drowned in the Red sea.", insight:"Egypt's chosen captains sank in the Red Sea. Human greatness, however chosen, cannot stay afloat against God."},
{q:"How did the Egyptians sink beneath the depths?", options:["As a stone","As a leaf","As dust","As smoke"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 15:5",verse:"The depths have covered them: they sank into the bottom as a stone.", insight:"They sank like a stone: heavy, final, irrecoverable. Sin's weight always pulls down what pride lifts up."},
{q:"What is glorious in power?", options:["Thy right hand, O LORD","Thy holy mountain","Thy chosen people","Thy pillar of fire"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 15:6",verse:"Thy right hand, O LORD, is become glorious in power: thy right hand, O LORD, hath dashed in pieces the enemy.", insight:"God's right hand is glorious in power. The song fixes our eyes on His strength, not on our weakness."},
{q:"What did the enemy boast he would do?", options:["I will pursue, I will overtake, I will divide the spoil","I will burn their tents","I will dam the sea","I will make peace"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 15:9",verse:"The enemy said, I will pursue, I will overtake, I will divide the spoil; my lust shall be satisfied upon them; I will draw my sword, my hand shall destroy them.", insight:"The enemy's speech is all I will: pursue, overtake, divide. Pride's boasts drowned in God's single act of power."},
{q:"What did the LORD blow with to cover the enemy?", options:["His wind","His trumpet","His nostrils of fire","His mighty hand"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 15:10",verse:"Thou didst blow with thy wind, the sea covered them: they sank as lead in the mighty waters.", insight:"God simply breathed, and the sea obeyed. The breath that drowned an army is the breath that gives us life."},
{q:"How did they sink in the mighty waters?", options:["As lead in the mighty waters","As stones in a brook","As sand in a storm","As chaff in the wind"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 15:10",verse:"Thou didst blow with thy wind, the sea covered them: they sank as lead in the mighty waters.", insight:"They sank like lead in mighty waters. What rises against God always sinks; His judgments have the weight of eternity."},
{q:"What question does the song ask about the LORD?", options:["Who is like unto thee, O LORD, among the gods?","Where is the LORD our God?","How long, O LORD?","Why hast thou forsaken us?"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 15:11",verse:"Who is like unto thee, O LORD, among the gods? who is like thee, glorious in holiness, fearful in praises, doing wonders?", insight:"Among the gods, none compares. The question is worship: once you see God's works, every rival fades."},
{q:"How had the LORD led forth His redeemed people?", options:["In His mercy","In His anger","In great haste","In secret"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 15:13",verse:"Thou in thy mercy hast led forth the people which thou hast redeemed: thou hast guided them in thy strength unto thy holy habitation.", insight:"Mercy led them, not merit. The redeemed are led by grace from the Red Sea to the promised rest."},
{q:"How long shall the LORD reign?", options:["For ever and ever","For a thousand years","Until the sea returns","For forty years"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 15:18",verse:"The LORD shall reign for ever and ever.", insight:"The song ends where all true theology ends: the LORD shall reign forever and ever. Every tyrant's reign has an expiry date; His does not."},
{q:"Who took a timbrel in her hand after the crossing?", options:["Miriam the prophetess, the sister of Aaron","Zipporah the wife of Moses","A daughter of Pharaoh","An Egyptian maidservant"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 15:20",verse:"And Miriam the prophetess, the sister of Aaron, took a timbrel in her hand; and all the women went out after her with timbrels and with dances.", insight:"Miriam is called a prophetess, leading worship with a timbrel. God gives women a voice in the song of redemption."},
{q:"What did the women go out with after her?", options:["Timbrels and dances","Harps and psalteries","Banners and trumpets","Palms and flowers"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 15:20",verse:"And Miriam the prophetess, the sister of Aaron, took a timbrel in her hand; and all the women went out after her with timbrels and with dances.", insight:"Worship went public: timbrels, dancing, procession. Joy too big for silence spilled into the streets."},
{q:"What was Miriam's refrain to the women?", options:["Sing ye to the LORD, for he hath triumphed gloriously","Dance before the ark of God","Shout, for the walls are fallen","Weep for the drowned host"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 15:21",verse:"And Miriam answered them, Sing ye to the LORD, for he hath triumphed gloriously; the horse and his rider hath he thrown into the sea.", insight:"Miriam's chorus answered Moses' song. Worship multiplies when God's people echo His praise back to one another."},
{q:"How long did Israel go in the wilderness of Shur finding no water?", options:["Three days","Forty days","Seven days","One day"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 15:22",verse:"So Moses brought Israel from the Red sea, and they went out into the wilderness of Shur; and they went three days in the wilderness, and found no water.", insight:"Three days without water tested the song. Praise is easy at the seashore; the wilderness asks whether we meant it."},
{q:"Why was the place called Marah?", options:["The waters were bitter","The people were angry","The trees were barren","The ground was cursed"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 15:23",verse:"And when they came to Marah, they could not drink of the waters of Marah, for they were bitter: therefore the name of it was called Marah.", insight:"They named it Marah, bitter, for the taste in their mouths. God lets us name our bitterness honestly before He heals it."},
{q:"What did the LORD show Moses to sweeten the waters?", options:["A tree, which he cast into the waters","A spring under a rock","A jar of honey","A branch of hyssop"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 15:25",verse:"And he cried unto the LORD; and the LORD shewed him a tree, which when he had cast into the waters, the waters were made sweet: there he made for them a statute and an ordinance, and there he proved them,", insight:"A tree cast into bitter waters made them sweet. Long before Calvary, God was already teaching that a tree can turn bitterness to sweetness."},
{q:"What did they find at Elim?", options:["Twelve wells of water, and seventy palm trees","A city with walls","Manna on the ground","A river of milk"], a:0, ref:"Exodus 15:27",verse:"And they came to Elim, where were twelve wells of water, and threescore and ten palm trees: and they encamped there by the waters.", insight:"Twelve wells and seventy palms: God provides rest with abundance. After Marah's bitterness, Elim's shade reminded them who leads."}
]}
]);
